1	// pattern: Imperative Shell
2	
3	//! The account-holder's notification endpoints:
4	//!
5	//! - `POST /v1/notifications/register` — register this device for push
6	//! - `DELETE /v1/notifications/register/{deviceUuid}` — stop pushing to this device
7	//! - `GET /v1/notifications/sender-keys` — the set of keys to pin
8	//!
9	//! Auth is `auth::extractors::authenticate_access` (the DPoP-binding seam every access-token
10	//! verification must route through — `just auth-seam-check` enforces it), so these live on
11	//! the same-origin `/v1/*` surface alongside the other wallet endpoints.
12	//!
13	//! Registration keys on `(did, device_uuid)` rather than the `devices` table: `devices` rows
14	//! are deleted inside the DID-promotion transaction, so a registration anchored there would
15	//! die exactly when the account starts mattering. `device_uuid` is app-generated and stable
16	//! across reinstalls of the same install identity. The relay round trip is **not** in the
17	//! request path: registration is one of the first calls the wallet makes after promotion, and
18	//! making it wait on a third-party relay would put that relay's uptime in the onboarding
19	//! critical path for a feature whose entire failure mode is a missed banner. The row is
20	//! stored, the relay registration is enqueued, and the response is `{"status":"pending"}`.
21	//!
22	//! DELETE is idempotent (`deleted`/`absent`) and drops the relay handle; the DID comes from
23	//! the credential, so one account can never unregister another's device.
24	//!
25	//! `sender-keys` is the re-pin surface (active + retired keys, never revoked ones), and
26	//! **503s** whenever no usable key can be served — no master key, a failed lazy generation, or
27	//! stored material that will not decrypt under the current KEK. That check is one invariant at
28	//! the exit of `notifications::published_sender_keys` ("never publish an empty set") rather
29	//! than a guard per failure path: every route to an empty set ends identically for a client —
30	//! it pins nothing and then silently fails to verify everything it later receives.
31	//!
32	//! Register and `sender-keys` **501** when no relay is configured — the route exists, the
33	//! instance simply has not opted in — but DELETE stays open, so a device can still clean up
34	//! its row (and the operator's relay still be told to drop the handle) after the feature is
35	//! switched off; gating it would strand exactly the rows an operator most wants gone.
36	
37	use axum::{
38	    extract::{Path, State},
39	    http::{HeaderMap, Method, Uri},
40	    Json,
41	};
42	use serde::{Deserialize, Serialize};
43	
44	use crate::app::AppState;
45	use crate::auth::extractors::authenticate_access;
46	use crate::db::notifications as store;
47	use crate::notify_relay_client::{NotifyJob, RegistrationOwner};
48	use crate::routes::notification_views::{
49	    require_notifications_enabled, sender_keys_response, validate_registration_fields,
50	    SenderKeysResponse,
51	};
52	use common::{ApiError, ErrorCode};
