3	//! The sending side of the notification relay: `notify_device(state, did, payload)` and
4	//! `notify_admin_devices` turn a notification into one HPKE-sealed, length-quantized payload
5	//! *per registered device* and hand each to the relay worker. Inert when
6	//! `[notifications] relay` is unset (`AppState.notify_sender` is `None`), down to never
7	//! minting key material.
8	//!
9	//! Three properties this module is responsible for, in the order they matter:
10	//!
11	//! 1. **Confidentiality.** The relay and Apple both handle the payload, and neither may read
12	//!    it. Every send is sealed to one specific device's notification public key.
13	//! 2. **Authenticity.** HPKE `mode_auth` proves the payload came from this instance's sender
14	//!    key. A malicious relay can drop or duplicate a push; it cannot author one that renders
15	//!    as genuine. Sender keys are minted lazily, on first send or first `sender-keys` fetch
16	//!    (`active_sender_key`, `published_sender_keys`).
17	//! 3. **Metadata minimization.** Ciphertext length is visible to everyone on the path, and
18	//!    notification *length* betrays notification *kind*. Every payload is padded up to a
19	//!    bucket before sealing, computed against the serialized APNs body via the shared
20	//!    `notify_relay::protocol::apns_envelope` (see `pad_len_for`); a payload too large for
21	//!    the top bucket is dropped, never sent unpadded or truncated (see `seal_for`).
22	//!
23	//! Fan-out is one seal per registration, never one seal reused: HPKE seals to a single
24	//! recipient key, and reusing an encapsulated key across devices would be a different (and
25	//! weaker) protocol. The sealing key is loaded once per fan-out.
26	//!
27	//! Nothing here is fallible from a caller's perspective. Every path ends in an enqueue or a
28	//! log line, because a notification is a courtesy: a missed banner is recovered the next time
29	//! the app talks to Custos, whereas a trigger that fails because a push relay is unwell has
30	//! turned a courtesy into an outage.
31	
